  [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m — no modules, no bundler, no build step — while [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are CommonJS Node
  files that [38;5;153mapp.js[39m cannot reach. [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies, no scripts, and no test runner. So "work across the
  app" has no existing sharing mechanism to plug into; that's part of what we're designing.

  First question — the persistence fork. [1mWhat differs:[0m how long the stored identity survives, and who can read it. This
  is not cheap to change later: it sets the storage API, the lifetime semantics other forms will assume, and the
  security posture.

  - [1mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restart, shared across all tabs of the origin. Buys: "remembered" identity, the
    usual meaning of persist. Costs: readable by any script on the origin, so an XSS bug exposes it; never put a raw
    auth token here without accepting that. Rules out per-tab identities.
  - [1msessionStorage[0m — lives until the tab closes, isolated per tab. Buys: same API, much smaller blast radius, and
    different tabs can hold different users. Costs: closing the tab loses it, which may not match "persist."
  - [1mServer-set cookie[0m — the login response sets it, ideally [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m. Buys: the only option JS-based XSS can't read, and
    it's sent automatically with API calls. Costs: requires the backend that doesn't exist yet (the API is still a
    stub), and [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m means your own JS can't read the userId for display.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module variable, no storage. Buys: nothing to leak, simplest. Costs: gone on refresh, so it fails
    your "should persist" requirement outright.

  [1mMy recommendation: localStorage for the userId, with the explicit rule that no auth token goes there.[0m It matches
  "persist" as you described it, needs no backend, and keeps the userId readable for the other forms you mentioned. The
  security cost is real but bounded if what we store is an opaque user identifier rather than a credential.
